  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nLittle change to the synoptic pattern has occurred over the past 24 \nhours, leading to almost a carbon-copy of yesterday's weather. \nMorning showers have waned and cloud cover has thinned, allowing \ntemperatures to heat up. Dewpoints are slightly lower across north \ncentral Kansas where temperatures are in the upper 90s and low 100s, \nbut dewpoints remain in the low 70s across eastern Kansas where \ntemperatures are in the 90s. The net result is widespread heat \nindices around 105 degrees. \n\nA weak surface boundary sags into northern Kansas on Wednesday, \nleading to uncertainty in how hot temperatures will reach. While \ncloud debris and showers along the boundary across far northern \nKansas could keep conditions slightly cooler than forecast, heat \nindex values are expected to reach 102-105 degrees for at least a \ncouple of hours. Further south, expect another hot and humid day \nwith heat index values again in the 105-110 degree range. The \nsouthward progression of the front continues to slow, leading to \nincreasing confidence in hot and humid conditions holding for the \nmajority of the area through at least Friday. Given the prolonged \nperiod of heat indices of 103-110, an Extreme Heat Warning has been \nissued through Friday for areas along and south of a Concordia to \nHolton line. Far northern Kansas is progged to see slightly lower \ntemperatures/heat indices Thursday and Friday, but speed of the \nfront will need to be monitored for any potential northward \nexpansion of the Extreme Heat Warning.\n\nThe aforementioned surface boundary could spark some showers or \nstorms Wednesday afternoon/evening. Additional development occurs \nWednesday evening and evolves into an MCS overnight along the nose \nof the LLJ. Highest chances remain north of the forecast area into \nNebraska, but there is a chance some of this activity reaches into \nfar northern Kansas and produce some strong wind gusts and small \nhail. Periodic chances for showers and storms continue through the \nend of the week as the boundary holds across the area. Best chances \nwill be along the nose of the LLJ during the overnight hours; \nguidance highlights northern Kansas into Nebraska as the most likely \narea to be impacted. There is a low risk for strong to severe storms \nfrom any convection that does impact the area, but widespread severe \nweather is unlikely. A stronger cold front is progged to move \nthrough this weekend and knock temperatures back to near-normal, \nalthough the speed of this front has begun to slow as well.",
